---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Why pinning tags does not work · Refledger"
  description="A Git tag is a movable pointer. Pinning to a tag does not pin the code your workflow runs."
  canonicalPath="/why-pinning-tags-does-not-work"
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Why pinning tags does not work</h1>
    <p>
      GitHub Actions lets a workflow pin a dependency with <code>uses: owner/repo@v4</code>. That
      looks like a version pin. It is not. The <code>@v4</code> suffix names a Git ref. A ref is a
      pointer. Maintainers can move it.
    </p>
    <p>
      When the pointer moves, every workflow that still says <code>@v4</code> starts running
      different code on the next job. There is no separate approval step in GitHub for that move.
      Consumers learn about it after the fact, if they watch.
    </p>
    <h2>What the public record shows</h2>
    <p>
      The ACM REP 2026 paper measured how often Action tags move and how widely those tags are
      depended on. Floating major tags are common in published workflows. A move at the tip of a
      popular floating tag reaches far.
    </p>
    <p>
      In March 2025, tags under <code>tj-actions</code> were moved as part of a supply-chain
      incident. Workflows that pinned those tags by name, not by commit SHA, ran the new tip.
    </p>
    <p>
      In March 2026, the Trivy Action incident (CVE-2026-33634) again showed the same shape: a tag
      consumers trusted by name pointed somewhere else than they thought. Pinning the tag name was
      not the same as pinning the tree they reviewed.
    </p>
    <h2>What to do instead</h2>
    <p>
      Pin the full commit SHA, and keep the tag name only as a comment for humans:
    </p>
    <div class="pre-block">
      <pre><code>uses: owner/repo@0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567 # v4.2.2</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Refledger records where watched tags pointed, and when the tip changed, so you can check the
      binding history yourself. See <a href="/verify">Verify</a> and the per-action pages under
      <a href="/actions">Actions</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
